{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfyk3jQKdCb5zgE4hqGxc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. Your generator already knows where every figure is, so you don't need to recover that from pixels.\n\n**Check the geometry directly.** At the point where the generator places each figure, record its box (x, y, width, height, after any scaling or cropping) and the slide size. Then assert:\n\n- every figure box sits inside the slide, or inside its content region if you keep margins;\n- figure boxes don't overlap each other or text boxes, if that matters to you;\n- the aspect ratio wasn't distorted (the placed box ratio matches the source image ratio).\n\nThis check is exact. It has no thresholds to tune, never flags anti-aliasing or a white figure on a white background, and when it fails it names the slide and figure, e.g. \"fig 3 on slide 12 extends 0.4in past the right edge.\"\n\n**If the output is a .pptx,** you can also check it after the fact: every `<p:pic>` stores its offset and extent (`a:off`, `a:ext`) in EMUs, and the slide size is in `presentation.xml`. With python-pptx, compare `shape.left + shape.width` to `prs.slide_width` and the same for height. That catches anything the generator got wrong between computing a position and writing it out.\n\n**What geometry can't catch:**\n- Clipping inside the image file itself, such as a chart whose axis labels were cut off when it was exported. That's a problem with the source asset, so check it there: export with tight bounding boxes or padding.\n- Renderer quirks, like a font substitution pushing text past its box. Text overflow is the one case where rendering and looking still helps. Even then, compare the measured text size against the box rather than scanning pixels for edges.\n\nIf you still want a pixel check, keep it narrow: render each figure's known box, and make sure the image's own edge pixels appear at the slide boundary. Don't scan the whole slide for \"something near the edge.\" That narrow version needs no tuning, because the geometry tells it where to look.\n\nIf you share the generator code, I can show you where to add the bounds assertion."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":12162,"cache_read_input_tokens":26645,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":12162},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1","uuid":"0320e5a6-2277-4642-9774-5ab72641fd80","timestamp":"2026-10-13T06:13:22.343Z","request_id":"req_011Cfyk3jEeM6kEEUP4w8Kgm"}
